  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "sanitize_shallow",
    "file": "harfbuzz/src/hb-open-type.hh",
    "description": "`VarSizedBinSearchArrayOf<Type>::sanitize_shallow()` uses the record-size check in the wrong direction: `Type::static_size >= header.unitSize`. This accepts attacker-controlled `unitSize` values smaller than the minimum size required by `Type`, including zero. Its aggregate bounds check then validates only `nUnits * unitSize` bytes. Later, `bsearch()` steps by that undersized stride, casts the location to `Type`, and `LookupSegmentSingle::cmp()` reads fixed fields (`first`/`last`) outside the validated range. In this PoV the resulting invalid read occurs in `BEInt<unsigned short,2>::operator unsigned short()`. The sanitizer must reject `header.unitSize < Type::static_size` (i.e. require `header.unitSize >= Type::static_size`) before accepting the array.",
    "conditions": "A malformed font supplies a `kerx` format-6 subtable whose short row-index lookup is AAT lookup format 2. The lookup's `VarSizedBinSearchHeader` encodes `unitSize = 0` and a nonzero attacker-controlled `nUnits` (the PoV uses bytes interpreted as a large count), with the lookup header placed at the end of the font data and no segment records following it. During face/table sanitization, the reversed size comparison accepts zero, and `check_array(..., nUnits, 0)` checks a zero-byte range and succeeds. Shaping the harness text enables AAT kerning; `KerxSubTableFormat6::get_kerning()` queries the row lookup, `LookupFormat2::get_value()` invokes `bsearch()`, and because the stride is zero it treats the end-of-buffer `bytesZ` address as a `LookupSegmentSingle<HBUINT16>` and reads its fixed-width glyph bounds past the heap buffer."
  },
